{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785092247,"nonce":"DWVnTpTdHJ447+UGCa3th2zDU7A=","title":"ADR-0061: warm-tier space-bounded LRU eviction","message":"The deferred half of s5. ADR-0061's retention table promises the warm tier is\nbounded by **space** — evict least-recently-used — while cold is bounded by\n**time** (ADR-0050). s1 ships the tiering but NOT the eviction: it ships a\n`scarab_workspace_warm_used_bytes` gauge on `/metrics` and an ENOSPC path\nthat logs `warm tier full — serving from cold` rather than failing a Step.\nThat is deliberate — do not ship a `WarmTier` impl you cannot exercise.\n\nDepends on: s1.\nSplit from: the landed half of s5 (pin + graceful degradation).\n\n---\n\n\n`ObjectStore::list_objects` returns `modified_ms` — i.e. **least-recently-\n*written***. For immutable content-addressed blobs that is the wrong key: a\nblob written a year ago and read hourly would evict FIRST, and a blob written\nthis morning and never read again would survive. Content addressing means\nwrite time carries no information about usefulness.\n\nReal LRU needs an **access index**, which nothing in this repo has. Building\nit is the substance of this ticket, not the eviction loop. Options to weigh\n(none chosen):\n\n- a sidecar index (sled/sqlite) on the warm PV, updated on every read —\n  cheap, but it is a second durable thing on the data plane;\n- `atime`-style touch files on the local FS — free-ish, but relies on mount\n  options and adds a write per read;\n- an in-memory LRU with periodic checkpoint — loses ordering on restart, but\n  the cost of losing it is only a worse eviction choice, never a wrong\n  answer (a warm miss is slower, never wrong).\n\nWhichever wins, note that space accounting is **adapter detail plus a policy\nloop**, not domain logic — hence a new port rather than widening `Cas`.\n\n\nNew, in `crates/scarab-storage/src/tiered.rs`, next to `TieredCas`. **Only\nthe warm tier implements it**; cold is bounded by time (ADR-0050), never by\nspace.\n\n```rust\npub trait WarmTier: Send + Sync {\n    async fn used_bytes(\u0026self) -\u003e Result\u003cu64, StorageError\u003e;\n    async fn touch(\u0026self, blobs: \u0026[BlobHash]) -\u003e Result\u003c(), StorageError\u003e;\n    /// Least-recently-touched first: (hash, bytes, last_touch_unix_ms).\n    async fn eviction_candidates(\u0026self, limit: usize)\n        -\u003e Result\u003cVec\u003c(BlobHash, u64, i64)\u003e, StorageError\u003e;\n    async fn evict(\u0026self, blobs: \u0026[BlobHash]) -\u003e Result\u003cu64, StorageError\u003e;\n}\n```\n\n\nGoes in `scarab-server`, mirroring `crates/scarab-server/src/retention.rs` —\nthat is where ADR-0050's mark-sweep already lives, so the precedent is\nsettled and the shape (a periodic task on the composition root, not a method\non a port) is not up for re-litigation.\n\nInteraction to get right: a **pinned** Run's workspaces (\"keep this Run's\nworkspaces\", the landed half of s5) must be exempt from eviction, and\neviction must never evict a blob reachable from a tree the pin protects.\nThat is the same reachability question ADR-0050's mark walk answers, so\nreuse it rather than inventing a second walk.\n\n\n- Warm usage stays under a configured ceiling under sustained load, with the\n  gauge proving it.\n- Evicting a blob a later Step needs produces a **slower** Step, never a\n  failed one — i.e. the cold fall-through path is exercised by the test, not\n  assumed.\n- A pinned Run's snapshots survive an eviction pass that would otherwise\n  reclaim them.","files":null}]}